    <item>
      <title>OAuth migration endpoints and mailbox limits lead this Exchange update — 6 November 2026</title>
      <link>https://daily.exchange.admin.news/day/2026-11-06/</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://daily.exchange.admin.news/day/2026-11-06/</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 06 Nov 2026 08:15:00 GMT</pubDate>
      <description><![CDATA[<div style="max-width:760px;color:#11181d;font-family:Arial,sans-serif"><div style="margin:0 0 24px"><div style="margin:0 0 6px;color:#0d5745;font:700 11px/1.3 Arial,sans-serif;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase">Day in brief · 6 November 2026</div><h2 style="margin:0 0 12px;font:650 28px/1.2 Georgia,serif"><a href="https://daily.exchange.admin.news/day/2026-11-06/" style="color:#11181d;text-decoration:none">OAuth migration endpoints and mailbox limits lead this Exchange update</a></h2><p style="margin:0;color:#263030;font:16px/1.6 Arial,sans-serif">The period delivered two substantive administrator guides: one covering OAuth authentication for Microsoft 365 Mailbox Replication Service migrations and another documenting Exchange Online message-size limits. Message Center notices also cover a policy-controlled unified inbox in new Outlook, Outlook event duplication, and custom profile-card properties planned for January 2027.</p></div><div style="margin:0 0 20px;padding:0 0 18px;border-bottom:1px solid #d8d3c7"><table role="presentation" width="100%" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0" border="0" style="width:100%;border:0;border-collapse:collapse"><tr><td width="46" valign="top" style="width:46px;min-width:46px;padding:1px 8px 0 0;vertical-align:top;white-space:nowrap"><img src="https://daily.exchange.admin.news/assets/products/hybrid.svg" width="20" height="20" alt="" style="display:inline-block;width:20px!important;max-width:20px!important;height:20px!important;max-height:20px!important;object-fit:contain;vertical-align:middle"><span style="display:inline-block;width:5px"></span><img src="https://daily.exchange.admin.news/assets/topics/email-authentication.svg" width="16" height="16" alt="" style="display:inline-block;width:16px!important;max-width:16px!important;height:16px!important;max-height:16px!important;object-fit:contain;vertical-align:middle"></td><td valign="top" style="padding:0;vertical-align:top"><div style="margin:0 0 5px;color:#0d5745;font:700 11px/1.35 Arial,sans-serif;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">Hybrid · Email authentication and transport security</div><h3 style="margin:0 0 7px;font:600 18px/1.3 Georgia,serif"><a href="https://daily.exchange.admin.news/day/2026-11-06/#doc-2026-11-06-create-migration-endpoints-with-oauth-authentication-01" style="color:#11181d;text-decoration:none">OAuth authentication is documented for MRS migration endpoints</a></h3></td></tr></table><p style="margin:5px 0 0;color:#46514e;font:14px/1.55 Arial,sans-serif">A new Hybrid guide explains how Microsoft 365 Mailbox Replication Service can use OAuth for migrations between Microsoft 365 and on-premises Exchange. It covers Entra ID application registration and supports Exchange Server Subscription Edition or Exchange 2019 CU15 with the latest ESU, together with Microsoft 365 commercial cloud. Adoption requires Entra ID access, migration-domain configuration, and registration of the on-premises API application.</p></div><div style="margin:0 0 20px;padding:0 0 18px;border-bottom:1px solid #d8d3c7"><table role="presentation" width="100%" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0" border="0" style="width:100%;border:0;border-collapse:collapse"><tr><td width="46" valign="top" style="width:46px;min-width:46px;padding:1px 8px 0 0;vertical-align:top;white-space:nowrap"><img src="https://daily.exchange.admin.news/assets/products/mail-flow.svg" width="20" height="20" alt="" style="display:inline-block;width:20px!important;max-width:20px!important;height:20px!important;max-height:20px!important;object-fit:contain;vertical-align:middle"><span style="display:inline-block;width:5px"></span><img src="https://daily.exchange.admin.news/assets/topics/general.svg" width="16" height="16" alt="" style="display:inline-block;width:16px!important;max-width:16px!important;height:16px!important;max-height:16px!important;object-fit:contain;vertical-align:middle"></td><td valign="top" style="padding:0;vertical-align:top"><div style="margin:0 0 5px;color:#0d5745;font:700 11px/1.35 Arial,sans-serif;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">Mail flow · General</div><h3 style="margin:0 0 7px;font:600 18px/1.3 Georgia,serif"><a href="https://daily.exchange.admin.news/day/2026-11-06/#doc-2026-11-06-message-size-limits-in-exchange-online-02" style="color:#11181d;text-decoration:none">Exchange Online message-size guidance sets concrete defaults and limits</a></h3></td></tr></table><p style="margin:5px 0 0;color:#46514e;font:14px/1.55 Arial,sans-serif">The new mail-flow article documents 35 MB sending and 36 MB receiving defaults, configurable mailbox limits from 1 MB to 150 MB, and EAC and PowerShell configuration. It also explains how headers, encoding, attachments, and content conversion contribute to message overhead and the effective limit.</p></div><div style="margin:0 0 20px;padding:0 0 18px;border-bottom:1px solid #d8d3c7"><table role="presentation" width="100%" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0" border="0" style="width:100%;border:0;border-collapse:collapse"><tr><td width="46" valign="top" style="width:46px;min-width:46px;padding:1px 8px 0 0;vertical-align:top;white-space:nowrap"><img src="https://daily.exchange.admin.news/assets/products/exchange-online.svg" width="20" height="20" alt="" style="display:inline-block;width:20px!important;max-width:20px!important;height:20px!important;max-height:20px!important;object-fit:contain;vertical-align:middle"><span style="display:inline-block;width:5px"></span><img src="https://daily.exchange.admin.news/assets/topics/general.svg" width="16" height="16" alt="" style="display:inline-block;width:16px!important;max-width:16px!important;height:16px!important;max-height:16px!important;object-fit:contain;vertical-align:middle"></td><td valign="top" style="padding:0;vertical-align:top"><div style="margin:0 0 5px;color:#0d5745;font:700 11px/1.35 Arial,sans-serif;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">Exchange Online · General</div><h3 style="margin:0 0 7px;font:600 18px/1.3 Georgia,serif"><a href="https://daily.exchange.admin.news/day/2026-11-06/#MC1519843" style="color:#11181d;text-decoration:none">New Outlook adds a policy-controlled All Accounts inbox</a></h3></td></tr></table><p style="margin:5px 0 0;color:#46514e;font:14px/1.55 Arial,sans-serif">The All Accounts, or Unified Inbox, view in new Outlook for Windows lets users manage email from multiple accounts in one view without merging the underlying mailboxes. Administrators can enable or disable the feature through a policy in the Microsoft 365 Apps admin center and control whether users can override the setting.</p></div><div style="margin:0 0 20px;padding:0 0 18px;border-bottom:1px solid #d8d3c7"><table role="presentation" width="100%" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0" border="0" style="width:100%;border:0;border-collapse:collapse"><tr><td width="46" valign="top" style="width:46px;min-width:46px;padding:1px 8px 0 0;vertical-align:top;white-space:nowrap"><img src="https://daily.exchange.admin.news/assets/products/exchange-online.svg" width="20" height="20" alt="" style="display:inline-block;width:20px!important;max-width:20px!important;height:20px!important;max-height:20px!important;object-fit:contain;vertical-align:middle"><span style="display:inline-block;width:5px"></span><img src="https://daily.exchange.admin.news/assets/topics/general.svg" width="16" height="16" alt="" style="display:inline-block;width:16px!important;max-width:16px!important;height:16px!important;max-height:16px!important;object-fit:contain;vertical-align:middle"></td><td valign="top" style="padding:0;vertical-align:top"><div style="margin:0 0 5px;color:#0d5745;font:700 11px/1.35 Arial,sans-serif;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">Exchange Online · General</div><h3 style="margin:0 0 7px;font:600 18px/1.3 Georgia,serif"><a href="https://daily.exchange.admin.news/day/2026-11-06/#MC1492166" style="color:#11181d;text-decoration:none">Outlook is rolling out event duplication</a></h3></td></tr></table><p style="margin:5px 0 0;color:#46514e;font:14px/1.55 Arial,sans-serif">The new Duplicate event capability lets users create an event from an existing one without re-entering its details. Microsoft says rollout began in late September 2026 and is scheduled to finish by early December 2026; the notice calls for no administrator action or compliance changes.</p></div><div style="margin:0 0 20px;padding:0 0 18px;border-bottom:1px solid #d8d3c7"><table role="presentation" width="100%" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0" border="0" style="width:100%;border:0;border-collapse:collapse"><tr><td width="46" valign="top" style="width:46px;min-width:46px;padding:1px 8px 0 0;vertical-align:top;white-space:nowrap"><img src="https://daily.exchange.admin.news/assets/products/exchange-online.svg" width="20" height="20" alt="" style="display:inline-block;width:20px!important;max-width:20px!important;height:20px!important;max-height:20px!important;object-fit:contain;vertical-align:middle"><span style="display:inline-block;width:5px"></span><img src="https://daily.exchange.admin.news/assets/topics/general.svg" width="16" height="16" alt="" style="display:inline-block;width:16px!important;max-width:16px!important;height:16px!important;max-height:16px!important;object-fit:contain;vertical-align:middle"></td><td valign="top" style="padding:0;vertical-align:top"><div style="margin:0 0 5px;color:#0d5745;font:700 11px/1.35 Arial,sans-serif;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">Exchange Online · General</div><h3 style="margin:0 0 7px;font:600 18px/1.3 Georgia,serif"><a href="https://daily.exchange.admin.news/day/2026-11-06/#MC1193692" style="color:#11181d;text-decoration:none">Profile cards will support up to 10 third-party custom properties</a></h3></td></tr></table><p style="margin:5px 0 0;color:#46514e;font:14px/1.55 Arial,sans-serif">Starting in January 2027, administrators will be able to add up to 10 properties from external systems to Microsoft 365 profile cards through Microsoft Graph People connectors. The feature is off by default and requires administrator setup; later updates are planned for SAP SuccessFactors and Workday support.</p></div><p style="margin:24px 0 0"><a href="https://daily.exchange.admin.news/day/2026-11-06/" style="color:#0d5745;font:700 15px/1.4 Georgia,serif">View all updates for 6 November 2026 →</a></p></div>]]></description>
      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<div style="max-width:760px;color:#11181d;font-family:Arial,sans-serif"><div style="margin:0 0 24px"><div style="margin:0 0 6px;color:#0d5745;font:700 11px/1.3 Arial,sans-serif;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase">Day in brief · 6 November 2026</div><h2 style="margin:0 0 12px;font:650 28px/1.2 Georgia,serif"><a href="https://daily.exchange.admin.news/day/2026-11-06/" style="color:#11181d;text-decoration:none">OAuth migration endpoints and mailbox limits lead this Exchange update</a></h2><p style="margin:0;color:#263030;font:16px/1.6 Arial,sans-serif">The period delivered two substantive administrator guides: one covering OAuth authentication for Microsoft 365 Mailbox Replication Service migrations and another documenting Exchange Online message-size limits. Message Center notices also cover a policy-controlled unified inbox in new Outlook, Outlook event duplication, and custom profile-card properties planned for January 2027.</p></div><div style="margin:0 0 20px;padding:0 0 18px;border-bottom:1px solid #d8d3c7"><table role="presentation" width="100%" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0" border="0" style="width:100%;border:0;border-collapse:collapse"><tr><td width="46" valign="top" style="width:46px;min-width:46px;padding:1px 8px 0 0;vertical-align:top;white-space:nowrap"><img src="https://daily.exchange.admin.news/assets/products/hybrid.svg" width="20" height="20" alt="" style="display:inline-block;width:20px!important;max-width:20px!important;height:20px!important;max-height:20px!important;object-fit:contain;vertical-align:middle"><span style="display:inline-block;width:5px"></span><img src="https://daily.exchange.admin.news/assets/topics/email-authentication.svg" width="16" height="16" alt="" style="display:inline-block;width:16px!important;max-width:16px!important;height:16px!important;max-height:16px!important;object-fit:contain;vertical-align:middle"></td><td valign="top" style="padding:0;vertical-align:top"><div style="margin:0 0 5px;color:#0d5745;font:700 11px/1.35 Arial,sans-serif;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">Hybrid · Email authentication and transport security</div><h3 style="margin:0 0 7px;font:600 18px/1.3 Georgia,serif"><a href="https://daily.exchange.admin.news/day/2026-11-06/#doc-2026-11-06-create-migration-endpoints-with-oauth-authentication-01" style="color:#11181d;text-decoration:none">OAuth authentication is documented for MRS migration endpoints</a></h3></td></tr></table><p style="margin:5px 0 0;color:#46514e;font:14px/1.55 Arial,sans-serif">A new Hybrid guide explains how Microsoft 365 Mailbox Replication Service can use OAuth for migrations between Microsoft 365 and on-premises Exchange. It covers Entra ID application registration and supports Exchange Server Subscription Edition or Exchange 2019 CU15 with the latest ESU, together with Microsoft 365 commercial cloud. Adoption requires Entra ID access, migration-domain configuration, and registration of the on-premises API application.</p></div><div style="margin:0 0 20px;padding:0 0 18px;border-bottom:1px solid #d8d3c7"><table role="presentation" width="100%" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0" border="0" style="width:100%;border:0;border-collapse:collapse"><tr><td width="46" valign="top" style="width:46px;min-width:46px;padding:1px 8px 0 0;vertical-align:top;white-space:nowrap"><img src="https://daily.exchange.admin.news/assets/products/mail-flow.svg" width="20" height="20" alt="" style="display:inline-block;width:20px!important;max-width:20px!important;height:20px!important;max-height:20px!important;object-fit:contain;vertical-align:middle"><span style="display:inline-block;width:5px"></span><img src="https://daily.exchange.admin.news/assets/topics/general.svg" width="16" height="16" alt="" style="display:inline-block;width:16px!important;max-width:16px!important;height:16px!important;max-height:16px!important;object-fit:contain;vertical-align:middle"></td><td valign="top" style="padding:0;vertical-align:top"><div style="margin:0 0 5px;color:#0d5745;font:700 11px/1.35 Arial,sans-serif;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">Mail flow · General</div><h3 style="margin:0 0 7px;font:600 18px/1.3 Georgia,serif"><a href="https://daily.exchange.admin.news/day/2026-11-06/#doc-2026-11-06-message-size-limits-in-exchange-online-02" style="color:#11181d;text-decoration:none">Exchange Online message-size guidance sets concrete defaults and limits</a></h3></td></tr></table><p style="margin:5px 0 0;color:#46514e;font:14px/1.55 Arial,sans-serif">The new mail-flow article documents 35 MB sending and 36 MB receiving defaults, configurable mailbox limits from 1 MB to 150 MB, and EAC and PowerShell configuration. It also explains how headers, encoding, attachments, and content conversion contribute to message overhead and the effective limit.</p></div><div style="margin:0 0 20px;padding:0 0 18px;border-bottom:1px solid #d8d3c7"><table role="presentation" width="100%" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0" border="0" style="width:100%;border:0;border-collapse:collapse"><tr><td width="46" valign="top" style="width:46px;min-width:46px;padding:1px 8px 0 0;vertical-align:top;white-space:nowrap"><img src="https://daily.exchange.admin.news/assets/products/exchange-online.svg" width="20" height="20" alt="" style="display:inline-block;width:20px!important;max-width:20px!important;height:20px!important;max-height:20px!important;object-fit:contain;vertical-align:middle"><span style="display:inline-block;width:5px"></span><img src="https://daily.exchange.admin.news/assets/topics/general.svg" width="16" height="16" alt="" style="display:inline-block;width:16px!important;max-width:16px!important;height:16px!important;max-height:16px!important;object-fit:contain;vertical-align:middle"></td><td valign="top" style="padding:0;vertical-align:top"><div style="margin:0 0 5px;color:#0d5745;font:700 11px/1.35 Arial,sans-serif;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">Exchange Online · General</div><h3 style="margin:0 0 7px;font:600 18px/1.3 Georgia,serif"><a href="https://daily.exchange.admin.news/day/2026-11-06/#MC1519843" style="color:#11181d;text-decoration:none">New Outlook adds a policy-controlled All Accounts inbox</a></h3></td></tr></table><p style="margin:5px 0 0;color:#46514e;font:14px/1.55 Arial,sans-serif">The All Accounts, or Unified Inbox, view in new Outlook for Windows lets users manage email from multiple accounts in one view without merging the underlying mailboxes. Administrators can enable or disable the feature through a policy in the Microsoft 365 Apps admin center and control whether users can override the setting.</p></div><div style="margin:0 0 20px;padding:0 0 18px;border-bottom:1px solid #d8d3c7"><table role="presentation" width="100%" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0" border="0" style="width:100%;border:0;border-collapse:collapse"><tr><td width="46" valign="top" style="width:46px;min-width:46px;padding:1px 8px 0 0;vertical-align:top;white-space:nowrap"><img src="https://daily.exchange.admin.news/assets/products/exchange-online.svg" width="20" height="20" alt="" style="display:inline-block;width:20px!important;max-width:20px!important;height:20px!important;max-height:20px!important;object-fit:contain;vertical-align:middle"><span style="display:inline-block;width:5px"></span><img src="https://daily.exchange.admin.news/assets/topics/general.svg" width="16" height="16" alt="" style="display:inline-block;width:16px!important;max-width:16px!important;height:16px!important;max-height:16px!important;object-fit:contain;vertical-align:middle"></td><td valign="top" style="padding:0;vertical-align:top"><div style="margin:0 0 5px;color:#0d5745;font:700 11px/1.35 Arial,sans-serif;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">Exchange Online · General</div><h3 style="margin:0 0 7px;font:600 18px/1.3 Georgia,serif"><a href="https://daily.exchange.admin.news/day/2026-11-06/#MC1492166" style="color:#11181d;text-decoration:none">Outlook is rolling out event duplication</a></h3></td></tr></table><p style="margin:5px 0 0;color:#46514e;font:14px/1.55 Arial,sans-serif">The new Duplicate event capability lets users create an event from an existing one without re-entering its details. Microsoft says rollout began in late September 2026 and is scheduled to finish by early December 2026; the notice calls for no administrator action or compliance changes.</p></div><div style="margin:0 0 20px;padding:0 0 18px;border-bottom:1px solid #d8d3c7"><table role="presentation" width="100%" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0" border="0" style="width:100%;border:0;border-collapse:collapse"><tr><td width="46" valign="top" style="width:46px;min-width:46px;padding:1px 8px 0 0;vertical-align:top;white-space:nowrap"><img src="https://daily.exchange.admin.news/assets/products/exchange-online.svg" width="20" height="20" alt="" style="display:inline-block;width:20px!important;max-width:20px!important;height:20px!important;max-height:20px!important;object-fit:contain;vertical-align:middle"><span style="display:inline-block;width:5px"></span><img src="https://daily.exchange.admin.news/assets/topics/general.svg" width="16" height="16" alt="" style="display:inline-block;width:16px!important;max-width:16px!important;height:16px!important;max-height:16px!important;object-fit:contain;vertical-align:middle"></td><td valign="top" style="padding:0;vertical-align:top"><div style="margin:0 0 5px;color:#0d5745;font:700 11px/1.35 Arial,sans-serif;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">Exchange Online · General</div><h3 style="margin:0 0 7px;font:600 18px/1.3 Georgia,serif"><a href="https://daily.exchange.admin.news/day/2026-11-06/#MC1193692" style="color:#11181d;text-decoration:none">Profile cards will support up to 10 third-party custom properties</a></h3></td></tr></table><p style="margin:5px 0 0;color:#46514e;font:14px/1.55 Arial,sans-serif">Starting in January 2027, administrators will be able to add up to 10 properties from external systems to Microsoft 365 profile cards through Microsoft Graph People connectors. The feature is off by default and requires administrator setup; later updates are planned for SAP SuccessFactors and Workday support.</p></div><p style="margin:24px 0 0"><a href="https://daily.exchange.admin.news/day/2026-11-06/" style="color:#0d5745;font:700 15px/1.4 Georgia,serif">View all updates for 6 November 2026 →</a></p></div>]]></content:encoded>
